>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><em>L5/L6 Level Technical Interv=
iew</em></p>
<hr>
<h2 class=3D"text-xl font-bold text-text-100 mt-1 -mb-0.5">Part 1: Warm-up =
&amp; Introduction</h2>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Interviewer</strong>: Go=
od morning! I'm Sarah, a Staff Engineer on the React Core team here at Meta=
. I've been working on frontend infrastructure for about 12 years now. Than=
ks for taking the time to interview with us today. Could you start by givin=
g me a brief overview of your background and what excites you about fronten=
d engineering?</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Candidate</strong>: Good=
 morning Sarah! Thanks for having me. I'm excited to be here. I've been wor=
king as a Senior Frontend Engineer for about 7 years, with the last 4 years=
 focused heavily on React ecosystems at scale. Currently, I'm leading front=
end architecture for a platform that serves over 50M daily active users.</p=
>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">What really excites me about fro=
ntend engineering is the intersection of <strong>performance optimization a=
nd user experience</strong>. I love diving deep into how browsers work, opt=
imizing rendering pipelines, and building abstractions that make complex st=
ate management feel intuitive for other engineers. Recently, I've been part=
icularly interested in <strong>concurrent features</strong> and how we can =
leverage React 18's capabilities to build more responsive UIs while maintai=
ning excellent developer experience.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Interviewer</strong>: Th=
at's great to hear. I see you mentioned scale - that's definitely something=
 we care about here. Before we dive deeper, I'm curious about your experien=
ce with state management. We use a variety of approaches here at Meta. What=
's your philosophy around choosing between different state management solut=
ions?</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Candidate</strong>: That=
's a really important question, and I think the key is <strong>matching the=
 tool to the problem domain</strong>. I approach this with a few core princ=
iples:</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>For component-local stat=
e</strong>: I always start with <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-=
0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] =
px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">useState</code> and <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 =
border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rou=
nded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">useReducer</code>. People often rea=
ch for global state too quickly when local state would suffice.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>For server state</strong=
>: I strongly prefer <strong>React Query or SWR</strong> over storing serve=
r data in Redux or Zustand. The caching, invalidation, and synchronization =
features are invaluable. At my current company, migrating from Redux-stored=
 API data to React Query reduced our bundle size by 30% and eliminated enti=
re classes of stale data bugs.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>For global client state<=
/strong>: This is where I evaluate based on complexity:</p>
<ul class=3D"[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:p=
b-1 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-7">
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Zustand</strong> for si=
mple global state - lightweight, TypeScript-friendly, and great DevEx</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Jotai</strong> when I n=
eed atomic state management or fine-grained reactivity</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Redux Toolkit</strong> =
for complex state machines or when I need time-travel debugging</li>
</ul>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>For forms</strong>: I ty=
pically reach for <strong>React Hook Form</strong> with <strong>Zod</strong=
> for validation. The uncontrolled approach performs much better than tradi=
tional controlled components at scale.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">The key insight I've learned is =
that <strong>most applications need multiple state management approaches</s=
trong>. The mistake is trying to force everything through one solution.</p>
<hr>
<h2 class=3D"text-xl font-bold text-text-100 mt-1 -mb-0.5">Part 2: Deep Tec=
hnical Discussion</h2>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Interviewer</strong>: Ex=
cellent perspective on state management. Let's dive deeper into something f=
undamental. Can you walk me through what happens when a user enters a URL i=
n the address bar and presses Enter? I'm particularly interested in the fro=
ntend aspects, but feel free to cover the full stack.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Candidate</strong>: Abso=
lutely! This is a fascinating journey that touches multiple layers. Let me =
break it down comprehensively:</p>
<h3 class=3D"text-lg font-bold text-text-100 mt-1 -mb-1.5">1. URL Parsing &=
amp; Validation</h3>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">First, the browser parses the in=
put to determine if it's a valid URL or a search query. It checks the proto=
col scheme, validates the hostname, and <strong>encodes any special charact=
ers</strong>. If the URL is malformed, most browsers will pass it to the de=
fault search engine.</p>
<h3 class=3D"text-lg font-bold text-text-100 mt-1 -mb-1.5">2. Cache Checkin=
g</h3>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">The browser performs multiple ca=
che checks:</p>
<ul class=3D"[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:p=
b-1 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-7">
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Memory cache</strong> (=
fastest, lost on tab close)</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Disk cache</strong> (pe=
rsistent across sessions)</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Service Worker cache</s=
trong> (programmable, great for offline-first apps)</li>
</ul>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">It checks <code class=3D"bg-text=
-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-w=
rap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">Cache-Control</code> headers=
, <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-dan=
ger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">ETag=
</code> values, and <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-b=
order-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px t=
ext-[0.9rem]">Last-Modified</code> timestamps to determine if cached resour=
ces are still fresh.</p>
<h3 class=3D"text-lg font-bold text-text-100 mt-1 -mb-1.5">3. DNS Resolutio=
n</h3>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">This is where it gets interestin=
g from a performance perspective:</p>
<ul class=3D"[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:p=
b-1 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-7">
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">Browser checks its <strong>DNS =
cache</strong> first</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">Then queries the <strong>OS DNS=
 cache</strong></li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">Falls back to <strong>recursive=
 DNS queries</strong> through ISP resolvers</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">Eventually reaches authoritativ=
e nameservers</li>
</ul>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Performance optimization=
</strong>: We can prefetch DNS with <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border bor=
der-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4r=
em] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">&lt;link rel=3D"dns-prefetch" href=3D"//examp=
le.com"&gt;</code> or use <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 bo=
rder-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 p=
y-px text-[0.9rem]">&lt;link rel=3D"preconnect"&gt;</code> for critical res=
ources.</p>
<h3 class=3D"text-lg font-bold text-text-100 mt-1 -mb-1.5">4. TCP Connectio=
n Establishment</h3>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">The <strong>three-way handshake<=
/strong>:</p>
<ol class=3D"[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:p=
b-1 list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-7">
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">Client sends SYN packet with in=
itial sequence number</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">Server responds with SYN-ACK</l=
i>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">Client sends ACK, connection es=
tablished</li>
</ol>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Modern optimization</str=
ong>: HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 reduce connection overhead through multiplexing and=
 QUIC protocol respectively.</p>
<h3 class=3D"text-lg font-bold text-text-100 mt-1 -mb-1.5">5. TLS Handshake=
 (for HTTPS)</h3>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">This is critical for security bu=
t adds latency:</p>
<ol class=3D"[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:p=
b-1 list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-7">
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>ClientHello</strong> wi=
th supported cipher suites</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>ServerHello</strong> wi=
th chosen cipher + certificate</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Certificate verificatio=
n</strong> by client</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Key exchange</strong> a=
nd <strong>session establishment</strong></li>
</ol>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Frontend optimization</s=
trong>: We can use <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-bo=
rder-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px te=
xt-[0.9rem]">&lt;link rel=3D"preconnect"&gt;</code> to start TLS handshakes=
 early.</p>
<h3 class=3D"text-lg font-bold text-text-100 mt-1 -mb-1.5">6. HTTP Request/=
